"""Audited, progressive teaching for chapters 17–24.

Examples teach components; none is a completed practice or local-lab solution.
Local-only framework/terminal illustrations are explicitly marked shell=True.
"""


def section(title, text, code=None, output=None, walk=None, check=None, shell=False):
    item = dict(title=title, text=text)
    if code is not None:
        item["code"] = code
    if output is not None:
        item["output"] = output
    if walk:
        item["walk"] = walk
    if check:
        item["check"] = check
    if shell:
        item["shell"] = True
    return item


CHAPTERS = {
    17: [
        section("Start here: a client, a server, and a contract",
                "An API is an interface through which one program asks another for data or an operation. A client sends the request; a server receives it and sends a response. HTTP is the message protocol used by many APIs. A request describes a method, address, headers, and sometimes a body. The method expresses the operation: GET usually reads, POST often creates or processes, PATCH partially updates, and DELETE removes.\n\nA response contains an integer status code, headers, and a body. Headers are named metadata such as Content-Type; the body is the actual content. JSON is common, but an error page might be HTML or plain text. This chapter's browser exercises use supplied values instead of contacting the internet. That lets you test the rules without an account, outage, or changing service data.",
                code='request = {"method": "GET", "path": "/books", "headers": {"Accept": "application/json"}}\nresponse = {"status": 200, "body": \'{"count": 2}\'}\nprint(request["method"], request["path"])\nprint(response["status"])',
                output="GET /books\n200",
                check="Which parts describe the operation, and which describe its result?"),
        section("Status codes: transport success is not valid application data",
                "The status is a number with a documented meaning. 2xx means an HTTP success, 3xx a redirect or related redirection response, 4xx a client-side request or access issue, and 5xx a server-side failure. 404 means a resource was not found; 429 means requests are being limited. These categories describe the protocol result, not who deserves blame. A legitimate request can receive 404, and a 200 body can still have the wrong fields.\n\nPython's chained comparison 200 <= status < 300 means both 200 <= status and status < 300. Follow the exercise contract exactly: a particular practice task may accept only 200 even though other 2xx statuses exist. Do not broaden the contract without deciding what every successful status's body means.",
                code='for status in [200, 204, 404, 503]:\n    successful_http = 200 <= status < 300\n    print(status, successful_http)',
                output="200 True\n204 True\n404 False\n503 False",
                check="Why might attempting to decode the body of a 204 response fail?"),
        section("Decode JSON only at the correct boundary",
                "json.loads(text) decodes JSON text into a Python value. The plural-looking loads means load from a string; it does not perform a network request. Invalid JSON raises json.JSONDecodeError. Valid JSON can produce a dictionary, list, number, string, boolean, or None. Decoding alone does not establish that a title field exists.\n\nCheck the status before handling a success body. Then decode, check the outer type, and validate required fields before using them. Do not call .get on a list, or .strip on a number. The example separates decoding from checking the shape rather than hiding both steps inside one expression.",
                code='import json\ntext = \'{"label": "Map room"}\'\ndata = json.loads(text)\nprint(isinstance(data, dict))\nlabel = data.get("label")\nprint(isinstance(label, str))\nprint(label.strip())',
                output="True\nTrue\nMap room",
                walk=["loads creates a dictionary from the JSON string.", "isinstance checks that dictionary methods are appropriate.", "The label value is checked separately before string operations."],
                check="Choose an invalid JSON string and a valid JSON list. How should their failures differ?"),
        section("Build query parameters with urlencode",
                "A URL has a scheme such as https, a host such as example.com, a path such as /search, and optionally a query after ?. A query contains name/value pairs joined by &. A literal ampersand inside a search phrase must be encoded so it is not mistaken for a separator.\n\nfrom urllib.parse import urlencode imports one standard-library helper. Give urlencode a dictionary and it returns the encoded query text. In that text, spaces commonly become + and a literal & becomes %26. Encoding preserves the structure; it does not make a URL trustworthy or provide authentication. The dictionary's insertion order determines the order of these parameters.",
                code='from urllib.parse import urlencode\nparameters = {"author": "Lee & Kim", "edition": 2}\nquery = urlencode(parameters)\nprint(query)\nprint("https://example.com/books?" + query)',
                output="author=Lee+%26+Kim&edition=2\nhttps://example.com/books?author=Lee+%26+Kim&edition=2",
                check="Which ampersand separates parameters, and which represents part of an author's name?"),
        section("A fixture is controlled example data",
                "A fixture is known data supplied to a test. It can represent a response without a real connection. A fake fetch function accepts the same kind of argument as the real fetch function but returns a fixture. Passing that function into other code is dependency injection: the caller chooses how the dependency works. No special framework is required.\n\nKeep the transformation separate from the request library. You can then simulate a success, missing resource, invalid schema, or failure on the second request. A test should assert the visible result, not merely that the fake was called. Reset mutable fixtures between tests or copy records before changing them.",
                code='def fixture_lookup(path):\n    return {"status": 200, "body": {"name": "Archive"}}\n\ndef show_status(fetcher):\n    response = fetcher("/location")\n    return response["status"]\n\nprint(show_status(fixture_lookup))',
                output="200",
                walk=["fixture_lookup without parentheses passes the function itself.", "fetcher(path) calls whichever fetch function was supplied.", "The example performs no HTTP operation."],
                check="How would you supply a 503 fixture without changing show_status?"),
        section("Pagination: continue only while progress is possible",
                "Pagination divides a large result into pages. An API may return items plus a next token: an opaque value identifying the next page. Opaque means use the token as documented, rather than guessing its internal structure. None often means there is no next page, but check the service contract.\n\nA client needs several independent stopping rules: no next token, a repeated token, a page limit, or a failed request. Track visited tokens outside the loop. A set's .add(token) remembers the token, and token in visited tests membership. Seeing the same token twice indicates lack of progress; blindly requesting it forever would be a bug. The exercise containing a list of pages focuses on processing supplied pages; the local client adds actual fetching.",
                code='tokens = ["page-a", "page-b", "page-b"]\nvisited = set()\nfor token in tokens:\n    if token in visited:\n        print("stopped: repeated token")\n        break\n    visited.add(token)\n    print("visit", token)',
                output="visit page-a\nvisit page-b\nstopped: repeated token",
                check="What second limit would stop a server that generates endless different tokens?"),
        section("Nested loops and stable identifiers",
                "Pages are often a list of lists. The outer loop selects one page; the inner loop selects a record from that page. An id is a stable identifier, not a list index: the record can move between pages while retaining its identity. Decide explicitly whether the first or last occurrence wins.\n\nRemembering identifiers across the entire run prevents overlaps from appearing twice. A set created inside the outer loop forgets previous pages. Use a list for ordered output and a separate set for membership; a set alone cannot promise first-seen display order. This is a design pattern to apply independently to your practice contract.",
                code='pages = [["opening", "middle"], ["ending"]]\nfor page_number, page in enumerate(pages, start=1):\n    for title in page:\n        print(page_number, title)',
                output="1 opening\n1 middle\n2 ending",
                walk=["enumerate supplies a page number and one page list.", "The inner loop finishes all titles on page 1 before page 2 begins."],
                check="Where must a remembered-id collection live if duplicates can cross page boundaries?"),
        section("Retry budgets and exponential backoff",
                "An attempt is one request. A retry is an additional attempt after a failure. A maximum of three attempts allows at most two waits before retries. Backoff means waiting before trying again; exponential backoff multiplies the wait each time. ** is Python's exponent operator, so 2 ** 0 is 1, 2 ** 1 is 2, and 2 ** 2 is 4.\n\nAn offline exercise calculates delays and does not actually sleep. Only record a delay when another attempt will occur. A retry policy should decide which failures are temporary and whether repeating the operation is safe. A GET read is different from creating a payment: a response can be lost after the server already completed the write. A fixed limit is necessary even for retryable failures.",
                code='waits = []\nfor retry_number in range(3):\n    waits.append(2 ** retry_number)\nprint(waits)\nprint("three waits permit four total attempts")',
                output="[1, 2, 4]\nthree waits permit four total attempts",
                check="If the very first request succeeds, how many delays should you record?"),
        section("Local Requests adapter: response methods and exceptions",
                "Requests is a third-party HTTP library, so install it into this chapter's environment rather than Forge's browser runtime. In an activated environment run python -m pip install requests, then record the installed version in your project's dependency file. On Windows you can replace python with .\\.venv\\Scripts\\python.exe if the environment is not active; on macOS/Linux use ./.venv/bin/python.\n\nFor a request you choose from a service's documentation, requests.get(url, params=..., timeout=(3, 10)) encodes query parameters and sets connect/read timeouts in seconds. A read timeout limits waiting for network activity; it is not a total-operation deadline. response.status_code is an attribute. response.raise_for_status() raises for HTTP error statuses; response.json() decodes the body and may fail separately. Catch requests.exceptions.Timeout and requests.exceptions.ConnectionError when handling those expected failures. Do not catch every exception and call the result success. No live request is needed to complete the browser practice.",
                code='python -m pip install requests\npython -m pip show requests',
                shell=True,
                check="Before a live request, identify the documented URL, required parameters, rate limit, and timeout policy."),
        section("Configuration, secrets, and an honest failure result",
                "The base URL is the shared address prefix, while a token is a credential. Keep both configurable; keep credentials out of committed source, screenshots, and logs. os.environ.get('SERVICE_TOKEN') reads a process environment variable or returns None if absent. Reading an environment variable does not create it. Use a synthetic fixture with no credential for the default demo.\n\nDecide what a failed middle page means. You might return an explicit partial result with errors, or fail the operation without claiming completeness. Document that choice and test it. Do not silently return the first page as if every page succeeded. For the lab, write tests covering repeated tokens, overlapping ids, invalid data, and a timeout through a fake fetcher before connecting Requests.",
                code='configuration = {"base_url": "https://example.com", "mode": "fixture"}\nprint(configuration["mode"])\nprint("Use fixture data; no credential required.")',
                output="fixture\nUse fixture data; no credential required.",
                check="What visible evidence would distinguish a complete result from a partial one?"),
    ],
    18: [
        section("Local scope and keyword arguments",
                "A function call receives its own parameters and local variables. A local assignment does not overwrite a caller's variable just because the names match. Mutating a supplied list is different: both caller and function may refer to the same object. Recall this distinction when refactoring.\n\nPositional arguments match parameters by position. Keyword arguments name them explicitly, as width=4. Defaults allow callers to omit an argument. After using a keyword argument, do not place an ordinary positional argument after it. Clear keyword names make multi-option calls easier to understand, but the names become part of your public interface.",
                code='def frame(width, border="*"):\n    return border * width\n\nwidth = 7\nprint(frame(width=4, border="-"))\nprint(width)',
                output="----\n7",
                check="Which width belongs to the function call, and which belongs to its caller?"),
        section("Safe defaults: omitted input is not shared state",
                "Python evaluates a default expression when it executes def, not every time the function is called. A default list therefore stays attached to the function and can retain mutations between calls. Use None to mean 'not provided', then create the fresh list inside the call. is None tests that exact sentinel; if not items would also match a supplied empty list.\n\nDecide whether your helper should mutate a supplied collection. Returning old_items + [item] constructs a new list; old_items.append(item) changes the existing one and returns None. Neither choice is automatically correct: document the intended contract and test two calls with no argument as well as a call supplying an existing list.",
                code='def envelope(message, tags=None):\n    if tags is None:\n        tags = []\n    return {"message": message, "tags": tags + ["sent"]}\n\nprint(envelope("first"))\nprint(envelope("second"))',
                output="{'message': 'first', 'tags': ['sent']}\n{'message': 'second', 'tags': ['sent']}",
                check="Why does creating a list inside the function avoid a hidden connection between calls?"),
        section("Docstrings and type annotations are different tools",
                "A docstring is the first string inside a function or class. Describe inputs, return meaning, possible errors, and mutation. Triple quotes allow a string to span lines. Type annotations such as text: str and -> int document expected types for readers and checking tools. Python does not automatically reject a wrong argument merely because a function has an annotation.\n\nlist[str] means a list whose elements are expected to be strings. int | None means an integer or None. A colon inside a parameter list introduces an annotation; a colon ending the def line starts its body. These uses have different roles. Run a type checker separately if your project chooses one, and still validate untrusted runtime input.",
                code='def character_count(text: str) -> int:\n    """Return the character count without changing the input."""\n    return len(text)\n\nprint(character_count("lamp"))\nprint(character_count.__doc__)',
                output="4\nReturn the character count without changing the input.",
                check="Would annotations alone stop somebody passing an integer to this function?"),
        section("Fresh instances and composition",
                "An instance is one object made from a class. Values assigned to self inside __init__ belong to that instance. A mutable class attribute would instead be shared through the class unless overridden; that can accidentally connect unrelated objects. Use independent instances for independent state.\n\nComposition means an object uses another object to do a job. For example, a notifier can use a recorder supplied by its caller. The notifier need not inherit from the recorder or know whether it records in memory or a file. This makes it possible to test with an in-memory substitute. Agree on the small method interface first.",
                code='class MemoryRecorder:\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.messages = []\n\n    def save(self, message):\n        self.messages.append(message)\n\nclass Notifier:\n    def __init__(self, recorder):\n        self.recorder = recorder\n\n    def announce(self, text):\n        self.recorder.save(text)\n\nrecord = MemoryRecorder()\nnotifier = Notifier(record)\nnotifier.announce("ready")\nprint(record.messages)',
                output="['ready']",
                walk=["MemoryRecorder creates its own messages list.", "Notifier stores a reference to the supplied recorder.", "announce calls that dependency's save method."],
                check="What method would a file-backed recorder need to support for this notifier?"),
        section("Modules and the import guard",
                "A module is a .py file. Importing it executes its top-level statements, then makes defined names available. Put reusable rules in a module that does not start a CLI or network request merely because a test imports it.\n\nPython sets __name__ to '__main__' when a file is the entry point, and usually to its module name when imported. The familiar if __name__ == '__main__': guard calls main only for direct execution. Double underscores here are part of the special name. Split modules by responsibility—parsing, rules, storage, output—when that helps somebody find and test behavior, rather than making a file for every function.",
                code='def entry_message():\n    return "Application entry point"\n\nif __name__ == "__main__":\n    print(entry_message())',
                output="Application entry point",
                check="Which statements in an imported file still run even when its main call is guarded?"),
        section("An iterator gives you one value at a time",
                "An iterable is something a for loop can read, such as a list or range. iter(iterable) obtains an iterator that remembers progress. next(iterator) asks for one value. After the final value, another next normally raises StopIteration; next(iterator, fallback) instead returns a chosen fallback.\n\nA list can provide a new iterator every time, but a particular iterator does not automatically rewind. This distinction matters when tests read a stream twice. Materializing with list(iterator) consumes all remaining values into a list and uses memory for them.",
                code='letters = iter(["a", "b"])\nprint(next(letters))\nprint(next(letters))\nprint(next(letters, "finished"))\nprint(list(letters))',
                output="a\nb\nfinished\n[]",
                check="Why does the final list contain no letters?"),
        section("yield creates a generator, not a returned list",
                "A function containing yield is a generator function. Calling it creates a generator object; its body starts when somebody requests a value. yield hands out a value and pauses, retaining local state. The next request resumes after that yield. return ends the generator.\n\nA generator can avoid an intermediate list, but its consumer may still keep every result. A loop uses it lazily; list(generator) requests all values. A file line iterator follows the same consume-as-needed idea. Start with a simple trace rather than assuming that shorter code is faster.",
                code='def positions():\n    for value in range(2, 7, 2):\n        yield value\n\nstream = positions()\nprint(next(stream))\nprint(list(stream))\nprint(list(stream))',
                output="2\n[4, 6]\n[]",
                walk=["range(start, stop, step) produces 2, 4, 6; stop 7 is excluded.", "next consumes only 2.", "The first list consumes what remains; the second sees an exhausted generator."],
                check="How would you obtain a fresh generator after exhausting this one?"),
        section("Slices and generator expressions",
                "values[start:stop] creates a list slice from start up to but excluding stop. A stop beyond the list's length is allowed. range(0, length, step) generates starting positions separated by step; step must not be zero. Decide what an invalid size means before using it in range.\n\nA generator expression resembles a list comprehension but uses parentheses: (value * 3 for value in values). It yields results as requested rather than building a list immediately. sum(expression) can consume these numeric values directly. This syntax is an optional shorthand; you can write an explicit loop first.",
                code='values = ["p", "q", "r", "s", "t"]\nprint(values[3:9])\nprint(list(range(0, len(values), 2)))\nprint(sum(number * 3 for number in [1, 2, 3]))',
                output="['s', 't']\n[0, 2, 4]\n18",
                check="Which operation creates a new list, and which operation requests values one by one?"),
        section("Configuration: distinguish presence from truthiness",
                "Configuration precedence says which source wins when two sources supply the same setting. A dictionary's .update(other) copies other's keys into that dictionary and replaces existing values; it mutates its receiver and returns None. Make a copy first when the original must stay unchanged.\n\nTest key membership when an explicitly supplied False, 0, or None has meaning. value or default is a truthiness fallback; it cannot distinguish an omitted key from a supplied false value. Python's {**mapping} syntax unpacks dictionary entries into a new dictionary, and later entries with the same key win. Read and test the longer .copy/.update version before using multiple ** expressions.",
                code='options = {"sound": True, "volume": 8}\nlocal = options.copy()\nlocal.update({"sound": False, "volume": 0})\nprint(local)\nprint(options)\nprint({**{"theme": "light"}, **{"theme": "dark"}})',
                output="{'sound': False, 'volume': 0}\n{'sound': True, 'volume': 8}\n{'theme': 'dark'}",
                check="Why would local.get('volume') or 8 lose a valid setting?"),
        section("Function arguments can be collected and unpacked",
                "Before reading decorators, learn the star syntax they often use. In def collect(*args, **kwargs), *args collects extra positional arguments into a tuple and **kwargs collects keyword arguments into a dictionary. The names args and kwargs are conventions; the stars determine the behavior.\n\nAt a call site, the direction is reversed: function(*values) passes collection elements as positional arguments, and function(**options) passes dictionary entries as keyword arguments. Do not use this flexibility when a fixed signature would be clearer. A wrapper uses it when it needs to forward a function's arguments without changing them.",
                code='def describe(*args, **kwargs):\n    print(args)\n    print(kwargs)\n\ndescribe("map", 2, color="blue")\n\ndef rectangle(width, height):\n    return width * height\n\nprint(rectangle(*[3, 4]))\nprint(rectangle(**{"width": 2, "height": 5}))',
                output="('map', 2)\n{'color': 'blue'}\n12\n10",
                check="Which star usage collects values, and which unpacks them?"),
        section("Decorators: wrap a function in small steps",
                "A function is a value you can assign, pass, and return. A decorator accepts a function and returns a replacement callable. The wrapper can call the original before or after adding behavior. A nested function can remember names from its enclosing function; that remembered environment is called a closure.\n\nWriting @decorate above a def applies the decorator when the function is defined. It is equivalent to name = decorate(name). First understand that explicit assignment. functools.wraps(original), used above a production wrapper, copies useful function metadata such as its name and docstring; it is not needed to understand the behavior in this small example.",
                code='def with_brackets(original):\n    def wrapped(name):\n        text = original(name)\n        return "[" + text + "]"\n    return wrapped\n\ndef welcome(name):\n    return "Hi " + name\n\nwelcome = with_brackets(welcome)\nprint(welcome("Mina"))',
                output="[Hi Mina]",
                walk=["with_brackets receives the welcome function without calling it.", "It returns wrapped, a new function that remembers original.", "The final call uses the wrapper, which calls the remembered original."],
                check="Where does the original greeting run, and where are brackets added?"),
        section("Dataclasses, properties, and validation",
                "A dataclass is a class for named fields. @dataclass asks Python to generate methods including initialization, a useful text representation, and field-based equality. Field annotations state intended types; ordinary dataclasses do not enforce them at runtime. A default after = is used when the field is omitted. For a mutable dataclass field, use dataclasses.field(default_factory=list) rather than a shared list.\n\n@property makes a method readable like an attribute: parcel.total instead of parcel.total(). Its value is recalculated when read, so it can reflect current fields. It does not automatically validate those fields. Decide whether a simple class, dictionary, or dataclass best explains your record; this is a tool, not a requirement for every function.",
                code='from dataclasses import dataclass\n\n@dataclass\nclass Parcel:\n    weight: int\n    fee_per_unit: int = 2\n\n    @property\n    def total(self):\n        return self.weight * self.fee_per_unit\n\nparcel = Parcel(3)\nprint(parcel)\nprint(parcel.total)\nparcel.weight = 4\nprint(parcel.total)',
                output="Parcel(weight=3, fee_per_unit=2)\n6\n8",
                check="Which decorator creates record methods, and which changes how total is accessed?"),
        section("Context managers and cleanup",
                "A with statement uses a context manager to perform setup and cleanup. It calls __enter__ before the block and __exit__ as the block ends, including when an exception leaves the block. In with manager as value, value receives the result of __enter__. __exit__ receives information about an exception; returning False lets it propagate rather than suppressing it.\n\nCleanup behavior depends on the manager. A file manager closes its file; a SQLite connection context commits or rolls back a transaction and does not itself close the connection. Do not generalize one manager's behavior to every with block. Abrupt process termination can prevent Python cleanup from completing. You rarely need to implement these methods yourself; use an existing manager when appropriate.",
                code='class Notice:\n    def __enter__(self):\n        print("enter")\n        return "workspace"\n\n    def __exit__(self, kind, error, trace):\n        print("leave")\n        return False\n\nwith Notice() as label:\n    print(label)',
                output="enter\nworkspace\nleave",
                check="What would label contain if __enter__ returned None?"),
        section("Apply the chapter without overengineering",
                "Refactoring changes structure while preserving the promised behavior. Before moving code, record existing CLI outputs and run regression tests. Then separate only responsibilities that make a concrete difference: argument parsing, core calculations, storage, and presentation. An injected dependency or settings record can help tests, but adding every technique in this chapter would make a small script harder to follow.\n\nFor the lab, keep the public command and output stable. Move one responsibility at a time, rerun tests, and compare actual output. Document configuration precedence and input mutation. Demonstrate streaming only where consuming one record at a time helps; a generator that is immediately converted to a list still stores all of those results.",
                check="Which current function mixes a rule with file access, and what small boundary would let you test that rule alone?"),
    ],
    19: [
        section("Recursion: smaller calls and returning answers",
                "Recursion means a function calls itself. A base case handles an input without making another call; a recursive case reduces the problem toward the base case. Each call gets its own local variables and waits for the smaller call's result. Trace both the downward calls and the upward returns.\n\nThe example counts characters recursively to make the control flow visible. text[1:] is a slice omitting the first character. The empty string ends the calls. This is a teaching example, not an improvement over len: repeated string slicing costs extra time and memory. Python limits nesting depth, so correct recursion can still fail for sufficiently deep input; RecursionError is not proof that the base case is wrong.",
                code='def letter_depth(text):\n    if text == "":\n        return 0\n    return 1 + letter_depth(text[1:])\n\nprint(letter_depth("sun"))',
                output="3",
                walk=["'sun' waits for 1 + letter_depth('un').", "'un' waits for 1 + letter_depth('n').", "'n' waits for 1 + letter_depth('').", "The empty string returns 0; pending calls then return 1, 2, and 3."],
                check="What progress measure decreases on every recursive call?"),
        section("Nested data and type-based decisions",
                "Nested data contains collections inside collections. Before recursing, decide which values are leaves and which values contain children. isinstance(value, list) asks whether the value is a list. A list might be empty; its loop simply runs zero times. Make the allowed leaf types explicit rather than assuming every non-list value is a valid number.\n\nA stack is an alternative way to remember pending work. A recursive call stack stores that work implicitly; an explicit list stack stores it visibly. list.pop() removes and returns the final item. Do not modify the collection you are currently iterating over unless the algorithm deliberately accounts for it.",
                code='pending = ["cover", ["page", "index"]]\nwhile pending:\n    value = pending.pop()\n    if isinstance(value, list):\n        for child in reversed(value):\n            pending.append(child)\n    else:\n        print(value)',
                output="page\nindex\ncover",
                walk=["pop takes the nested list first because it is last on the stack.", "reversed visits its children backward so subsequent pops restore their left-to-right order.", "The original 'cover' item remains pending until both children are processed."],
                check="How does a stack's last-in-first-out behavior affect output order?"),
        section("Trees, graphs, vertices, and directed edges",
                "A vertex, or node, is an item in a graph; an edge is a connection. In a directed graph, a connection from A to B does not automatically imply a connection from B to A. A dictionary can map each node to a list of outgoing neighbors; this is an adjacency list. graph.get(node, []) treats a missing key as having no outgoing neighbors.\n\nA tree is a connected graph without cycles, with additional direction or root conventions depending on representation. Graphs in general can revisit a node through cycles or multiple routes. A traversal must therefore remember visited nodes. The number of edges along a route is a distance only when every edge has equal cost.",
                code='routes = {"hall": ["desk", "stairs"], "desk": ["hall"]}\nprint(routes["hall"])\nprint(routes.get("stairs", []))\nprint("hall" in routes["desk"])',
                output="['desk', 'stairs']\n[]\nTrue",
                check="Which two dictionary entries form a cycle?"),
        section("deque: a queue that removes from the front",
                "A queue processes the oldest waiting item first: first in, first out. collections.deque is a double-ended queue. deque([...]) constructs one, .append(value) adds on the right, and .popleft() removes and returns the leftmost item. Removing from an empty deque raises IndexError, so a while queue loop checks that items remain.\n\nA list's pop(0) also removes the front but shifts later elements. deque.popleft is designed for efficient front removal. A queue item can be a tuple containing a node and its distance. node, distance = item unpacks that two-part tuple into two names; it does not make two queue removals.",
                code='from collections import deque\nwaiting = deque([("door", 0)])\nwaiting.append(("desk", 1))\nwhile waiting:\n    node, distance = waiting.popleft()\n    print(node, distance)',
                output="door 0\ndesk 1",
                check="Which item would a stack remove first from the same two entries?"),
        section("Breadth-first traversal: process one distance level first",
                "Breadth-first search, or BFS, uses a queue to explore nodes in increasing number of edges from the start. A node's unvisited neighbors join the back with distance one greater. Mark a neighbor visited when you enqueue it, so two parents cannot both queue it before it is processed.\n\nKeep the queue and seen collection outside the loop. If the queue becomes empty, all reachable nodes have been processed. A goal absent from that reachable set is unreachable. For adjacency lists, the usual traversal cost is O(V + E): each reachable vertex is queued once and each outgoing edge is examined once. BFS does not solve weighted shortest-route problems in general.",
                code='from collections import deque\nconnections = {"entry": ["left", "right"], "left": ["entry"], "right": []}\nwaiting = deque([("entry", 0)])\nseen = {"entry"}\nwhile waiting:\n    place, depth = waiting.popleft()\n    print(place, depth)\n    for neighbor in connections.get(place, []):\n        if neighbor not in seen:\n            seen.add(neighbor)\n            waiting.append((neighbor, depth + 1))',
                output="entry 0\nleft 1\nright 1",
                walk=["entry is seen before processing begins.", "Both neighbors join behind the current entry at depth 1.", "left's edge back to entry is skipped because entry is already seen."],
                check="Why would marking only on removal allow duplicate waiting items?"),
        section("Route reconstruction needs a predecessor map",
                "Finding a distance is different from returning the actual route. To reconstruct a route later, remember the predecessor that first discovered each node. A predecessor map stores child -> parent, with a sentinel such as None for the start. Follow these links backward from a found goal until the sentinel, then reverse the collected sequence.\n\nThe snippet shows only the stored relationship. You still need to design how your own search records it, what to return for an unreachable goal, and how start equal to goal behaves. In a grid, decide which moves are legal before translating cells into graph connections. Coordinate tuples can be dictionary keys because tuples of integers are hashable.",
                code='predecessor = {"gate": None, "bridge": "gate", "tower": "bridge"}\nprint(predecessor["tower"])\nprint(predecessor["bridge"])\nprint(predecessor["gate"])',
                output="bridge\ngate\nNone",
                check="Which order would you encounter these places while following links backward?"),
        section("Linked nodes are references, not neighboring list positions",
                "A singly linked node holds a value and a reference to the next node. None marks the end. A Python dictionary or a small Node class can represent one. next is a descriptive field name here, not a command that automatically traverses the chain. A loop must repeatedly assign its cursor to that next reference.\n\nTwo nodes can live anywhere in memory; their relationship comes from the stored reference. Reaching the third node requires following earlier links rather than indexing directly. For reversal, draw the chain and preserve the next reference before overwriting a link, or you can lose access to the rest. A cycle would prevent an ordinary until-None traversal from ending.",
                code='second = {"value": "end", "next": None}\nfirst = {"value": "start", "next": second}\nprint(first["value"])\nprint(first["next"]["value"])\nprint(second["next"])',
                output="start\nend\nNone",
                check="Does assigning another name to first copy the chain? Why not?"),
        section("A Node class and the reversal drawing",
                "The class form gives the same two fields attribute syntax: node.value and node.next. Each constructor call creates a fresh object. Class and dictionary nodes teach the same reference model; the practice contract specifies which representation it expects.\n\nBefore reversing three links, draw previous, current, and following. First save following = current.next; only then redirect current.next toward previous. Move the names forward after preserving the remainder. Apply this reasoning yourself in the lab rather than copying a memorized loop. Test an empty chain and a one-node chain too.",
                code='class Link:\n    def __init__(self, value, next_link=None):\n        self.value = value\n        self.next = next_link\n\nlast = Link("b")\nhead = Link("a", last)\nprint(head.value, head.next.value)\nprint(last.next is None)',
                output="a b\nTrue",
                check="Which reference must you save before changing a node's next field?"),
        section("Heaps: the smallest item is ready, not the whole list sorted",
                "heapq provides min-heap operations on a normal list. heappush(heap, item) inserts while maintaining heap rules. heappop(heap) removes and returns the smallest item. Both change the list. The internal list is not a fully sorted display; only the heap property is promised. heap[0] is the smallest while the heap is nonempty.\n\nUse repeated pops to obtain priority order. Adding or removing costs O(log n); reading the smallest costs O(1). Sorting a fixed batch once can be simpler, while a heap helps when work arrives dynamically and you repeatedly need the next priority.",
                code='import heapq\nwaiting = []\nheapq.heappush(waiting, 6)\nheapq.heappush(waiting, 2)\nheapq.heappush(waiting, 4)\nprint(waiting[0])\nprint(heapq.heappop(waiting))\nprint(heapq.heappop(waiting))',
                output="2\n2\n4",
                check="Why should a report not print the raw heap and call it sorted?"),
        section("Heap tuple keys and stable ties",
                "Tuple comparison examines components from left to right. A heap entry (priority, sequence, payload) compares priority first, sequence next, and payload only if both earlier values tie. Give every arrival a unique increasing sequence number when ties should keep arrival order. This also avoids asking Python to compare arbitrary payload objects.\n\nA name in the second position would instead break ties alphabetically. That might be valid for another contract, but it does not preserve arrival order. Choose the tie rule explicitly before choosing the entry shape. enumerate supplies a convenient unique sequence for a fixed input batch.",
                code='import heapq\nheap = []\nheapq.heappush(heap, (1, 0, "zebra"))\nheapq.heappush(heap, (1, 1, "apple"))\nprint(heapq.heappop(heap))\nprint(heapq.heappop(heap))',
                output="(1, 0, 'zebra')\n(1, 1, 'apple')",
                check="Why does zebra come before apple even though a sorts before z?"),
        section("Memoization is an optional extension with a contract",
                "Memoization stores answers for inputs already computed. functools.lru_cache is a decorator that can do this for functions with hashable arguments. maxsize=None permits an unbounded cache. The function should return the same meaningful result for the same arguments and should not depend on changing hidden state.\n\nCaching trades memory for avoided repeated computation; it does not make all recursion linear. Keep this separate from first learning recursion or BFS. Inspect the cache statistics to see whether inputs actually repeat. Do not cache a function whose intended behavior is to produce a fresh timestamp, perform a write, or read changing data without an invalidation plan.",
                code='from functools import lru_cache\n\n@lru_cache(maxsize=None)\ndef badge_width(text):\n    return len(text) + 2\n\nprint(badge_width("north"))\nprint(badge_width("north"))\nprint(badge_width.cache_info().hits)',
                output="7\n7\n1",
                check="Which resources grow if you cache millions of distinct inputs without a limit?"),
    ],
    20: [
        section("A route is an HTTP boundary around ordinary code",
                "A backend is a program listening for requests. Routing chooses a handler based on the HTTP method and URL path. The handler translates request data into domain inputs, calls your tested rules, and translates the result into a status and public response. A repository is the component that reads or writes stored records.\n\nKeep these responsibilities separate. A browser practice function returning a dictionary with a status field models a decision; it is not itself an HTTP server. The lab introduces a real local framework. Start with synthetic data and localhost, then connect your tested SQLite repository after you can verify one tiny route.",
                code='domain_result = {"available": 3}\nhttp_model = {"status": 200, "body": domain_result}\nprint(http_model["status"])\nprint(http_model["body"])',
                output="200\n{'available': 3}",
                check="Which part should still be testable when the web server is not running?"),
        section("Type checks before normalization",
                "Untrusted JSON may contain missing fields, None, booleans, or the wrong type. Read a field, check its type, then normalize. .strip removes outer whitespace from a string; calling it on None fails. A nonempty stripped string is different from a merely nonempty original string containing spaces.\n\nPython treats bool as a subclass of int: isinstance(True, int) is True. When the contract allows ordinary integers but excludes booleans, type(value) is int is an explicit exact-type check. Perform range checks only after the type is known. A validation model and a domain rule may use different conversion policies; document whether numeric strings are coerced or rejected.",
                code='values = [True, 0, "4", None]\nfor value in values:\n    print(repr(value), isinstance(value, int), type(value) is int)',
                output="True True False\n0 True True\n'4' False False\nNone False False",
                check="Why could a truthiness check accidentally reject zero while accepting True?"),
        section("Public fields and consistent errors",
                "An allowlist names exactly the fields a client may receive or edit. Construct a fresh public dictionary from those fields instead of serializing a storage row wholesale. Deleting one known secret field is fragile because a future internal field may then leak.\n\nUse a documented error shape and suitable HTTP status. 200 means ordinary success, 201 creation, 204 success without a response body, 401 missing or invalid authentication, 403 denied permission, 404 not found, 409 a conflict, and 422 a validation failure in common FastAPI responses. Do not use 500 as an intentional ordinary validation result. A tutorial's particular status policy is a contract to follow, not a universal rule for all APIs.",
                code='stored = {"code": 12, "label": "Workshop", "private_note": "staff only"}\npublic = {"code": stored["code"], "label": stored["label"]}\nprint(public)\nprint(stored["private_note"])',
                output="{'code': 12, 'label': 'Workshop'}\nstaff only",
                check="What would happen to a new internal field if you returned stored directly?"),
        section("Offset, limit, and nonmutating sorting",
                "An offset counts items to skip; a limit caps items to return. These are counts, not page numbers. A slice values[start:stop] excludes stop, so offset plus limit gives an end position. Sort consistently before selecting the slice, or pages can vary even when the dataset is unchanged.\n\nsorted(values) returns a new list; values.sort() changes the original and returns None. A negative offset in Python indexing normally counts from the end, so a contract that clamps offsets to zero requires an explicit decision before slicing. A limit of zero should follow the endpoint contract, rather than accidentally selecting the full list.",
                code='labels = ["west", "east", "north", "south"]\nordered = sorted(labels)\nprint(ordered)\nprint(ordered[1:3])\nprint(labels)',
                output="['east', 'north', 'south', 'west']\n['north', 'south']\n['west', 'east', 'north', 'south']",
                check="Which two items does an offset of 1 and limit of 2 select here?"),
        section("Partial updates: absent is different from False or null",
                "A PATCH-style partial update changes only supplied fields. If a key is missing, leave that value alone. An explicit False is still a supplied value. JSON null becomes Python None and may mean clear a field only if your contract allows it. Use key in changes to test presence; changes.get(key) by itself loses this distinction.\n\nStart from a copy when the original must be preserved, apply only editable fields, then validate the complete candidate before saving it. A shallow copy duplicates the outer dictionary; nested mutable values are still shared. When nested updates exist, choose and test a suitable copying or reconstruction policy.",
                code='change = {"enabled": False, "description": None}\nprint("enabled" in change, change["enabled"])\nprint("description" in change, change["description"] is None)\nprint("name" in change)',
                output="True False\nTrue True\nFalse",
                check="Why would if change.get('enabled') skip a requested change?"),
        section("Authentication and authorization are separate",
                "Authentication establishes an identity using a trusted mechanism. Authorization checks whether that authenticated identity can perform this action on this resource. A caller-provided owner id is not proof of identity. A hidden button is not access control: a client can send a request without using your interface.\n\nThe browser exercises teach permission rules using trusted supplied inputs. For the lab, a fake identity boundary makes route tests reproducible. Label it as practice-only. Do not present a hard-coded user or a header anybody can choose as production authentication. A real deployment should use maintained identity tooling and enforce permissions server-side for every relevant operation.",
                check="Which value in your service comes from a verified identity boundary, and which values come from the caller's payload?"),
        section("Install and start one local FastAPI example",
                "Run these commands in your activated week environment, not in a Python source file. fastapi[standard] requests FastAPI plus its standard development tools; quotes keep the brackets together in shells. httpx supports the test client shown later. Record tested dependency versions in your project rather than depending forever on whatever happens to be newest.\n\nNext create a separate local file named demo_api.py using the following section. The final command starts a local development server and keeps the terminal occupied. Open http://127.0.0.1:8001/notice in a browser; http://127.0.0.1:8001/docs opens interactive API documentation. The command deliberately uses port 8001 so it can run beside Forge on port 8000. Stop the server with Ctrl+C. This is a local teaching example, not your game-library lab solution.",
                code='python -m pip install "fastapi[standard]" httpx\npython -m pip show fastapi httpx\npython -m uvicorn demo_api:app --host 127.0.0.1 --port 8001',
                shell=True,
                check="What file and object does demo_api:app tell the server to load?"),
        section("Read the route decorator before running the server",
                "Save this illustration in demo_api.py. FastAPI() creates the application object. @app.get('/notice') registers the following function for GET requests at /notice; this uses the decorator idea from chapter 18. The decorator is configuration, not a call that runs the handler immediately. A regular def is enough for this example; async programming is not required here.\n\nReturning a dictionary allows the framework to serialize JSON and produce a response. Here code is a path parameter from the URL, and its int annotation asks FastAPI to parse and validate it. /notice/7 succeeds; /notice/not-a-number produces a validation response. The separate /notice route has no parameter. Compare this with an ordinary Python function annotation, which by itself does not validate calls.",
                code='from fastapi import FastAPI\n\napp = FastAPI()\n\n@app.get("/notice")\ndef notice():\n    return {"message": "Local demo"}\n\n@app.get("/notice/{code}")\ndef numbered_notice(code: int):\n    return {"code": code}',
                shell=True,
                walk=["The app object holds route registrations.", "{code} in the path captures a URL component.", "FastAPI uses the annotation at the request boundary; ordinary direct calls remain Python calls."],
                check="Which requests exercise a normal result and a validation failure?"),
        section("Request models and validation policy",
                "A request model describes named fields in a JSON body. Pydantic's BaseModel is the base class used by FastAPI for this purpose. Field annotations describe types; Field adds constraints such as a minimum length or a nonnegative value. StrictBool and StrictInt can reject coercions when your contract requires exact JSON types.\n\nFor example, class MessageBody(BaseModel): followed by text: str defines a required string field. A default makes a field optional to supply. body.model_dump() creates a dictionary from a model; model_dump(exclude_unset=True) keeps only explicitly supplied fields for a partial update. Do not confuse omitted fields with fields supplied as False or None. The local lab requires you to design its own request/response fields and reuse tested domain rules; a model does not replace every business check.",
                code='from pydantic import BaseModel, Field, StrictInt\n\nclass MessageBody(BaseModel):\n    text: str = Field(min_length=1)\n    priority: StrictInt = Field(ge=0)\n\nbody = MessageBody(text="Hello", priority=0)\nprint(body.model_dump())',
                shell=True,
                check="Does a minimum string length of one reject a string containing only spaces? What additional rule is needed?"),
        section("Test a route without launching a live server",
                "FastAPI's TestClient sends requests to your application in the test process. It exercises routing and validation without opening a public listening port. Import TestClient, pass the app, then call client.get or client.post. The result exposes status_code and .json(), matching concepts from HTTP clients. assert checks a condition and fails the test if it is false.\n\nFor the separate demo_api.py file above, save these assertions as a local test or try them in a separate script. These tests do not need network access. For your lab, add fixtures and a fresh temporary repository for each test; exercise invalid bodies, missing ids, denied operations, and successful cases. A fake user must be supplied through a deliberate test boundary, not trusted from arbitrary request data.",
                code='from fastapi.testclient import TestClient\nfrom demo_api import app\n\nclient = TestClient(app)\nresponse = client.get("/notice/7")\nassert response.status_code == 200\nassert response.json() == {"code": 7}\nassert client.get("/notice/not-a-number").status_code == 422',
                shell=True,
                check="Which of these assertions would catch accidentally removing the int annotation?"),
        section("Connect storage only after the boundary is understood",
                "A repository hides storage details behind a small interface such as find, list, or save. A route should not need to know SQL formatting. Use parameterized SQL from chapter 16 inside the repository, and commit related changes together. Public response models should allowlist fields instead of returning internal objects wholesale.\n\nTo finish the lab, document methods, routes, body examples, statuses, pagination limits, and authorization assumptions. Run both core and route tests. Try the API locally through /docs or a documented client request and record its actual response. Do not deploy this practice service as a secure multi-user application merely because its local tests pass.",
                check="Can you replace the persistent repository with a fresh in-memory test repository without changing the domain rule?"),
    ],
    21: [
        section("Dependable means reruns and failures are understandable",
                "An unattended batch job must report what it did, keep data consistent, and behave predictably when run again. Idempotent means applying the same operation twice has the same intended final effect as once. It does not mean the program never writes logs or that every counter must be identical. Decide which data state is promised.\n\nSeparate validation, planning, application, and reporting. A dry run executes the planning stage and describes proposed changes without writing them. A successful exit should mean the documented success condition was met, not just that an exception was swallowed. A partial failure needs a visible outcome and a recovery plan.",
                check="For your importer, does a duplicate id replace, merge, skip, or reject? Write the policy before implementing it."),
        section("Structured events before log formatting",
                "A structured event is a dictionary with stable field names: an event name, relevant counts, and a severity. INFO describes ordinary progress, WARNING highlights an unexpected or partially rejected condition, and ERROR describes a failure. Choose severity according to a documented policy, rather than printing every event as an error.\n\nKeep event construction testable without the logging system. An event should include enough context to investigate, such as a run identifier or row number, while excluding secrets and unnecessary personal data. A log message is evidence about an operation; it is not proof that every input was accounted for.",
                code='event = {"event": "scan_started", "run_id": "sample-1", "files": 3}\nprint(event["event"])\nprint(event["files"])',
                output="scan_started\n3",
                check="Which fields would help compare two runs without logging their entire input payloads?"),
        section("logging methods and formatting placeholders",
                "The standard logging module routes messages according to severity and configuration. logging.getLogger(name) obtains a named logger. .info, .warning, and .error emit at different levels. basicConfig commonly sets a minimum level and a display format at the application's entry point; libraries should generally leave overall configuration to their caller.\n\nlogger.warning('row %d: %r', index, value) supplies a format string and separate arguments. %d formats a number; %r uses repr, which shows quotes and escapes helpful for debugging. %(levelname)s and %(message)s in a logging configuration refer to event metadata. Do not confuse these placeholders with f-string braces. The snippet formats one record without changing the page's global logging configuration.",
                code='import logging\nrecord = logging.LogRecord("demo", logging.WARNING, "sample.py", 1, "row %d rejected", (4,), None)\nformatter = logging.Formatter("%(levelname)s %(message)s")\nprint(formatter.format(record))',
                output="WARNING row 4 rejected",
                check="Why should an application avoid logging a secret token even when a request fails?"),
        section("Upsert policies and copies of records",
                "Upsert combines update and insert. A stable id lets you find an existing record; if it is present, follow the update policy, otherwise insert a new one. A dictionary naturally associates one id with one value, but the code still needs an explicit first-wins or last-wins rule.\n\nCopy a supplied record when your result must not share the same mutable outer dictionary. A list copy alone leaves its record dictionaries shared. A shallow record copy is sufficient only when nested mutation is not part of the operation. Sorting ids before building a report makes output deterministic; arrival order and id order are different policies.",
                code='source = {"key": 8, "value": "draft"}\nregistry = {8: source.copy()}\nregistry[8]["value"] = "reviewed"\nprint(source["value"])\nprint(registry[8]["value"])',
                output="draft\nreviewed",
                check="What would happen if registry stored source directly instead of source.copy()?"),
        section("Regular expressions start with a tiny alphabet",
                "A regular expression is a pattern describing strings. re.fullmatch(pattern, text) succeeds only if the whole string matches; re.search can find a match inside a longer string. The return is a match object on success or None otherwise. bool(match) converts that result to True or False.\n\nSquare brackets describe one allowed character: [A-Z] means one uppercase ASCII letter. + repeats the preceding item one or more times; {2,4} means two through four repeats. A dot normally means any character, so \\. means a literal dot. r'...' is a raw Python string that keeps backslashes convenient for the regex parser. Learn these pieces independently before assembling the lab's filename policy.",
                code='import re\npattern = r"[A-Z]{2,4}\\.txt"\nfor name in ["AB.txt", "A.txt", "AB.txt.extra", "ab.txt"]:\n    print(name, bool(re.fullmatch(pattern, name)))',
                output="AB.txt True\nA.txt False\nAB.txt.extra False\nab.txt False",
                walk=["[A-Z]{2,4} allows a two-to-four-letter uppercase base.", "\\.txt requires the exact extension, including the dot.", "fullmatch rejects trailing content that a substring search might accept."],
                check="Which part of the pattern controls length, and which controls the permitted characters?"),
        section("Filename validation and actual filesystem boundaries",
                "A lexical filename rule validates text before it becomes a path. A strict allowlist is easier to reason about than trying to remove every dangerous fragment. Path traversal such as ../private.txt attempts to leave the intended directory. Windows also recognizes backslashes, so a forward-slash-only test is not a complete cross-platform policy.\n\nEven a well-formed base name does not establish where a write will occur. Join it to a controlled directory, consider resolved paths and symlinks, and test in temporary directories. pathlib.Path.resolve follows filesystem structure; PurePosixPath manipulates path syntax without proving a real location is safe. The browser regex exercise is deliberately a text-validation exercise, not a complete secure file writer.",
                code='from pathlib import PurePosixPath\npath = PurePosixPath("reports/summary.txt")\nprint(path.name)\nprint(path.parts)',
                output="summary.txt\n('reports', 'summary.txt')",
                check="Why does extracting .name from an untrusted path fail to prove the original input was acceptable?"),
        section("Account for every row exactly once",
                "A reconciliation report explains where every input went. Invalid rows and duplicate valid rows are different categories. Validate before deciding a row is an acceptable duplicate; a repeated id does not excuse a malformed title. When categories are exclusive and exhaustive, their counts add up to the input length.\n\nNormalize a string only after checking its type. Check exact integer type when booleans are excluded. Keep a rejection reason where useful, but avoid placing sensitive raw payloads in logs. A test should include invalid rows before and after a valid row with the same id, so the chosen policy is explicit.",
                code='counts = {"processed": 4, "rejected": 2, "skipped": 1}\naccounted = counts["processed"] + counts["rejected"] + counts["skipped"]\nprint(accounted)\nprint(accounted == 7)',
                output="7\nTrue",
                check="Would adding one row to two categories make the reconciliation misleading?"),
        section("Transactions and atomic replacement solve different problems",
                "A database transaction groups related database changes so they can commit together or roll back on failure. A uniqueness constraint can reinforce an id policy. Transactions do not automatically include unrelated filesystem writes or network calls. Record what is inside the transaction boundary and what is outside.\n\nFor a report file, write the complete result to a temporary sibling file, then replace the destination after the write succeeds. os.replace is designed to replace a destination atomically when the platform and filesystem support the operation; it does not by itself guarantee recovery from every power failure. Use a temp file on the same filesystem. Preserve original input so a rejected or failed batch can be investigated and rerun.",
                check="If the database commits but writing the report fails, what will your tool tell its caller?"),
        section("Test recovery by making failure happen on purpose",
                "A happy-path run cannot show that recovery works. Use a fake dependency that fails after a known number of operations, or an isolated temporary database, and inspect the resulting state. Do not test failure recovery against your only copy of valuable files.\n\nFor this lab, compare state after importing the same fixture twice, then force a failure partway through. Verify your documented all-or-nothing or partial-result policy. Test --dry-run by comparing state before and after; its output alone cannot prove that it avoided writes. Record actual counts and a recovery action in the README, rather than only 'it works'.",
                check="Which assertion would demonstrate that a dry run changed no stored records?"),
    ],
    22: [
        section("Map an unfamiliar project before editing",
                "Start by running the documented command and tests. Record pre-existing failures rather than claiming a clean baseline you did not observe. Locate the entry point, the parser or HTTP boundary, one domain function, storage, and output. Search for a relevant function's name and its callers instead of reading every file in order.\n\nA code map can be a short chain: command -> parser -> rule -> repository -> formatter. Follow one concrete input through it. Existing tests show expected behavior, but they may omit a boundary or encode an old requirement. Check the documented contract before calling surprising behavior a bug.",
                check="For an earlier project, name the file that starts execution and the function that decides the result you want to change."),
        section("Missing keys and explicit None are different states",
                "dictionary.get(key) returns None for an absent key unless a different default is given. That can hide the difference between {} and {'note': None}. Test membership with key in mapping when absence itself matters. Comparing dictionaries' values is not enough when the requirement distinguishes missing from explicit null.\n\nA schema is the agreed field names and types for a record. A migration translates old schema to new schema. Decide precedence by key presence, preserve valid False and 0, construct a fresh result when inputs must remain unchanged, and test both versions. Do not use a truthy fallback when an explicitly supplied zero should win.",
                code='old_record = {}\nnew_record = {"note": None}\nprint(old_record.get("note") == new_record.get("note"))\nprint("note" in old_record)\nprint("note" in new_record)',
                output="True\nFalse\nTrue",
                check="Which test distinguishes absence from an explicitly stored None?"),
        section("Type hints help readers; adapters preserve callers",
                "A function annotation describes a contract to readers and optional type-checking tools. record: dict means a dictionary is expected; -> str means a string result is intended. Runtime validation remains your responsibility. More specific annotations such as dict[str, int] describe key and value types but do not guarantee that data loaded from JSON obeys them.\n\nAn adapter accepts one interface or data shape and produces another. During a transition, keep compatibility intentionally rather than renaming a field and hoping every caller updates at once. Test the old record, the new record, a record containing both, and a new field whose value is zero or False. Document when the old form can be retired.",
                code='def measurement_label(value: int) -> str:\n    """Describe a measurement for presentation."""\n    return f"{value} units"\n\nprint(measurement_label(0))',
                output="0 units",
                check="If you change a public parameter name, which keyword-argument callers may break?"),
        section("Set union, intersection, and difference",
                "A set stores unique values. set(list_of_names) removes duplicates without promising output order. The | operator is union: values in either set. & is intersection: values present in both. A - B is difference: values in A but not B. Reversing the subtraction usually changes the result. sorted(set_result) gives a predictable list for a report.\n\nThese are useful for coverage and change comparisons. A requirement set defines what matters; unrelated completed work should not automatically become an accepted requirement. First write each category in ordinary words, then choose its operation. These operators on sets differ from arithmetic subtraction or bit operations in other contexts.",
                code='north = {"apple", "pear"}\nsouth = {"pear", "plum"}\nprint(sorted(north | south))\nprint(sorted(north & south))\nprint(sorted(north - south))\nprint(sorted(south - north))',
                output="['apple', 'pear', 'plum']\n['pear']\n['apple']\n['plum']",
                check="Which operation means 'in both', and why is difference directional?"),
        section("Regression tests establish the before-and-after behavior",
                "A regression test preserves a bug's smallest reproducing example so it cannot quietly return. First verify that the new test fails for the relevant reason on the old implementation. A syntax error or missing import is not the same evidence as an assertion showing the wrong behavior. Then make a focused change and confirm the test passes.\n\nRun related tests and the full relevant suite afterward. A refactor should preserve public behavior; a behavior change should state exactly what changes. Review the diff for unrelated edits and unexpected generated files. Keep your reproduction input and expected result with the change.",
                check="Can you demonstrate that your new test detects the original bug instead of passing both before and after?"),
        section("Branches and pull requests: a reviewable proposal",
                "A Git branch names a line of commits. git switch -c creates a branch and switches to it; it does not copy the working folder into a second directory. A pull request proposes merging that branch into a target branch and displays its diff. Creating or updating a PR can notify reviewers, so in practice use your own repository or an agreed collaboration workflow.\n\nRun these commands only in the intended learning repository after reviewing git status. git diff shows unstaged changes; git diff --staged shows what the next commit will contain. The example branch and commit describe a hypothetical documentation change, not a solution to the lab. After pushing, use GitHub's Compare & pull request action to review the proposal.",
                code='git status\ngit switch -c clarify-run-instructions\ngit diff\ngit add README.md\ngit diff --staged\ngit commit -m "Clarify local run instructions"\ngit push -u origin clarify-run-instructions',
                shell=True,
                check="Which command shows the exact README content selected for the commit?"),
        section("A useful PR description and review comment",
                "Describe the concrete problem, the resulting behavior, and the evidence. For example, explain which command previously failed, what it does now, and which test command passed. Record limitations such as a platform not tested. A screenshot alone rarely explains a reproducible bug.\n\nA useful review comment points to a consequence: 'This fallback discards a configured zero, so the zero case should be tested.' A preference such as 'I dislike this name' needs a clarity reason to be actionable. When receiving review, check the behavior and update the code or explain the tradeoff with evidence. Keep factual findings separate from guesses.",
                check="Write a three-sentence handoff: what was wrong, what changed, and how you checked it."),
        section("Conflicts require understanding both changes",
                "A merge conflict means Git could not combine overlapping edits automatically. The conflict markers show competing text, not instructions about which side is correct. Open both intended changes, decide the combined behavior, edit deliberately, and rerun tests. Removing markers without understanding the code can produce a syntactically valid but incorrect result.\n\nPractice only in a disposable branch or your own small repository with committed work. git status tells you which files need resolution. After resolving, stage the reviewed files and follow Git's instructions for completing or aborting the merge. Do not use force-push or discard commands as a shortcut around an unfamiliar conflict.",
                check="What two requirements must remain true after combining the competing changes?"),
        section("Failure triage is a comparison, not a diagnosis",
                "Triage sorts evidence into categories so you know where to investigate. A newly failing test was absent from the earlier failure set; a resolved failure disappeared; an unchanged failure is present in both. Set comparison can identify these groups, but it cannot tell you the cause of a failure. A service outage, flaky timing, changed fixture, or regression can look similar at first.\n\nFor your maintenance lab, preserve a baseline, give reproduction steps, add a regression test, and make one focused change. Report the test command, actual outcome, affected scope, and any existing failures. Complete a practice PR and conflict resolution only after the local behavior is understood.",
                check="How would you word a report that distinguishes an observed new failure from an unverified theory about its cause?"),
    ],
    23: [
        section("A complete workflow has an explicit boundary",
                "A portfolio project should solve a small real problem from input through output. Choose a coherent workflow, such as reading a synthetic collection, searching it, and writing a report. State the supported inputs and exact success criteria before adding more commands or an interface.\n\nA dispatcher selects an operation from a command name. Keep dispatch separate from printing and storage so unknown commands can be tested. A public representation should select fields intentionally rather than exposing everything in a database row. This chapter assembles earlier skills; it does not require a new clever algorithm.",
                check="What is the smallest end-to-end task another person can complete using your project?"),
        section("Make expected output and limitations reproducible",
                "A demo should use committed synthetic sample input with a documented command and actual output. A screenshot may be useful, but text input/output lets another person repeat the result. Explain ordering, empty input, invalid rows, and known limits. Keep generated reports and private databases out of source unless they are intentional fixtures.\n\nA README should state what the program does, the supported runtime, setup, dependencies, run commands, test commands, sample data, and limitations. Document the working directory for each command. Test your instructions literally rather than silently relying on an activated environment, a secret file, or a globally installed package.",
                check="Which undeclared file or global package could make the project work only on your current machine?"),
        section("Clean installs use the chosen interpreter",
                "A clean-install test starts from a separate clone or copy and a fresh virtual environment. Use the same interpreter for installation and execution: python -m pip calls pip associated with that python, unlike an ambiguous standalone pip command. The lab's explicit .venv interpreter paths work without activation.\n\nRun the commands below from a fresh project copy after creating its environment and activating it, or replace python with the environment's interpreter path. requirements.txt lists third-party runtime dependencies; built-in modules such as json and unittest do not need pip packages. If your project has no third-party dependencies, document that instead of inventing a requirements file. Record versions you tested, and review changes when updating them.",
                code='python --version\npython -m pip install -r requirements.txt\npython -m unittest -v\npython main.py',
                shell=True,
                check="Which commands should a reviewer run if your project uses only the standard library?"),
        section("Exit status is how automation detects failure",
                "A terminal command returns an integer exit status. Conventionally zero means success and nonzero means failure. Test runners return a failing status when assertions fail, so an automation system can stop without reading human text. A script that prints 'error' but exits zero may be mistaken for a successful job.\n\nIn Python, raise SystemExit(1) or sys.exit(1) requests a failing process exit; do this in the CLI boundary, not in a reusable core function. In PowerShell, $LASTEXITCODE reports the most recent native program's exit code. A CI summary should derive success from actual exit codes, not just log messages.",
                code='exit_codes = [0, 0, 2]\nfor code in exit_codes:\n    print(code, code == 0)',
                output="0 True\n0 True\n2 False",
                check="What exit status should your automated test command report after one failed assertion?"),
        section("all and generator expressions, read slowly",
                "all(iterable) returns True when every item is truthy, and stops at the first false item. For an empty iterable it returns True: there is no counterexample. If a contract requires the actual boolean True, compare with is True instead of merely trusting a value's truthiness. A string such as 'yes' is truthy but is not True.\n\nA generator expression can supply checks one by one. In all(flag is True for flag in flags), read the inner phrase as 'for each flag, produce whether it is exactly True'. Write a loop first if the one-line form is hard to trace. Do not assume all validates the meaning of an evidence note; it only combines the checks you supply.",
                code='print(all([True, True]))\nprint(all([]))\nprint(all(["yes", 1]))\nflags = [True, "yes"]\nprint(all(flag is True for flag in flags))',
                output="True\nTrue\nTrue\nFalse",
                check="Why can a list of truthy strings pass all without proving a boolean-only contract?"),
        section("CI runs your known checks in a fresh environment",
                "Continuous integration, or CI, automatically runs checks for changes. A job is a group of steps on a runner machine. Typical steps check out the source, select Python, install declared dependencies, and run the project's test command. The runner starts without your local .venv or uncommitted files, exposing hidden setup assumptions.\n\nCI is useful only when its checks detect real problems. A green badge cannot establish correctness for behaviors never tested. First make the commands reliable locally, then automate those same commands. Workflow execution and any service usage limits depend on the hosting plan and repository settings; check GitHub's current Actions documentation instead of assuming unlimited usage.",
                check="Which exact local test command should the runner execute, and what nonzero result should make the job fail?"),
        section("Read a GitHub Actions workflow before adapting it",
                "A GitHub Actions workflow is a YAML file under .github/workflows/. YAML uses indentation to nest mappings and - to begin list items. name labels the workflow; on chooses triggers; jobs groups named jobs; runs-on selects the runner; steps is an ordered list. uses invokes a reusable action, while run executes a shell command.\n\nSave the example as .github/workflows/python-checks.yml at the root of your Forge Work repository. It runs on pushes and pull requests. checkout downloads the committed files into the runner; setup-python selects Python 3.12. The @v6 and @v5 suffixes select action versions, not Python versions. These choices follow GitHub's documented Python examples; review maintained versions when updating your workflow. permissions: contents: read permits reading the repository.\n\nThe default working-directory is week-23 because this learning repository keeps code in chapter subfolders. Change that folder only if your project lives elsewhere. Every run step starts there, while uses steps operate on the repository. The example runs standard-library tests; if your project needs third-party packages, add a run step for python -m pip install -r requirements.txt before testing, with that dependency file in the same chapter folder. Check the Actions log reports the expected number of tests: a green run with zero tests is not evidence. Push a practice branch and inspect its Actions run before relying on the workflow.",
                code='name: Python checks\non: [push, pull_request]\npermissions:\n  contents: read\njobs:\n  tests:\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n    defaults:\n      run:\n        working-directory: week-23\n    steps:\n      - uses: actions/checkout@v6\n      - uses: actions/setup-python@v5\n        with:\n          python-version: "3.12"\n      - name: Run tests\n        run: python -m unittest discover -s . -v',
                shell=True,
                check="Why would this structural illustration need checkout before it could find your test files?"),
        section("Prove CI can fail, then restore the passing change",
                "On an isolated practice branch in your own repository, introduce one deliberately failing assertion and observe the failing job. Then repair it and observe a passing run for the repair commit. This checks that the runner discovers the tests and respects their exit status. Do not merge the deliberate failure into your main branch.\n\nDistinguish a test failure from infrastructure failure: a dependency download or missing interpreter can fail before any test executes. Read the job logs and verify how many tests ran. The lab evidence should name the branch or commit, command, and failing-then-passing outcome. Never put credentials in the workflow file or echo their values into a job log.",
                check="What log evidence proves tests were discovered rather than the command succeeding with zero tests?"),
        section("Versions are numeric components, not plain text ordering",
                "A version such as 2.10.3 has components. Under Semantic Versioning, a patch signals a compatible fix, a minor version compatible functionality, and a major version an incompatible public API change. That convention applies when a project adopts it; not every package follows it.\n\nSplit on the literal dot, convert components with int, then reason about numeric tuples. Lexicographic string order would place '2.10' before '2.9', which is misleading. A dependency pin improves repeatability but also needs an update and review policy. Do not claim that pinning alone makes an environment reproducible across every operating system and Python version.",
                code='parts = "2.10.3".split(".")\nnumbers = []\nfor part in parts:\n    numbers.append(int(part))\nmajor, minor, patch = numbers\nprint(major, minor, patch)\nprint((2, 10, 3) > (2, 9, 8))',
                output="2 10 3\nTrue",
                check="Which part of your README states the Python and dependency versions you actually tested?"),
        section("Evidence is stronger than a readiness checkbox",
                "For this lab, finish two small workflows using a tested core: the CLI workflow and an API or automation workflow. Keep sample data synthetic, verify a clean install, and demonstrate CI's ability to fail. Explain one design tradeoff and one bug found by a test.\n\nA release checklist records actions you have actually performed. A dictionary of True values only models that checklist; it cannot certify that a person understands the implementation. If you used generated code or outside help, record where and show you can explain, adapt, and debug it. Keep unfinished features and untested platforms visible rather than turning them into implied guarantees.",
                check="Can another person reproduce your main workflow using only the repository and README?"),
    ],
    24: [
        section("Start an unfamiliar task by clarifying its contract",
                "Before coding, identify inputs, output type, allowed invalid values, ordering, tie rules, and whether input may change. Clarifying is programming work: it prevents implementing a plausible answer to the wrong question. Write one ordinary example and one boundary example before choosing an algorithm.\n\nFor a timed rehearsal, spend a few minutes stating assumptions and then build the smallest coherent result. You can look up syntax while still doing the reasoning yourself. Record any generated solution or outside guidance honestly. The goal is evidence that you can adapt and debug, not repeat a remembered answer.",
                check="For a ranking request, what happens when two scores tie and when the requested limit is zero?"),
        section("Sorting keys: priority and tie order are separate decisions",
                "sorted(items, key=function) asks the key function for a comparable value for each item. The function is passed without calling it first. A tuple key compares its first component, then uses the next component to break ties. Negating a numeric component reverses its numeric direction while leaving a later string component ascending.\n\nA lambda is a small anonymous function: lambda word: len(word) means the same result as a named function returning len(word). Use a named def when you need comments or several steps. Sorting produces a new list. A final slice limits the ordered result; a negative slice endpoint has a real meaning in Python, so handle a contract's nonpositive limit explicitly rather than relying on slicing accidentally.",
                code='def word_order(word):\n    return (-len(word), word)\n\nwords = ["oak", "elm", "cedar"]\nprint(sorted(words, key=word_order))\nprint(sorted(words, key=lambda word: (-len(word), word)))',
                output="['cedar', 'elm', 'oak']\n['cedar', 'elm', 'oak']",
                check="Which tuple component makes longer words come first, and which orders equal lengths?"),
        section("Windows are contiguous ranges of positions",
                "A window is a contiguous region of a sequence. Contiguous means no skipped positions. Left and right indices describe its boundaries; if both ends are included, its length is right - left + 1. Python's slice syntax excludes its stop, so the matching slice is text[left:right + 1].\n\nA fixed-size window can slide one position at a time, dropping the old first item and adding the next item. A variable-size window grows or shrinks to maintain a rule. First identify that rule in words. Do not copy an update expression without explaining which positions it represents and why the window remains valid.",
                code='text = "planet"\nleft = 1\nright = 3\nprint(text[left:right + 1])\nprint(right - left + 1)\nfor start in range(len(text) - 2):\n    print(text[start:start + 3])',
                output="lan\n3\npla\nlan\nane\nnet",
                check="Why is the slice endpoint one greater than the included right index?"),
        section("Remember positions and move a boundary only forward",
                "A dictionary can associate a character with the last index where it appeared. enumerate(text) yields index/character pairs, so you can compare a remembered occurrence with the current window. An occurrence before left is outside the active window; it should not force left backward.\n\nThe monotonic rule is that left never decreases. If a constraint requires excluding an old position, the new boundary must be at least one past that position and at least its current value. max selects the larger bound. This is a boundary concept to apply in the practice task, not a complete implementation. Trace a repeated character inside the window and one whose old occurrence is already outside it.",
                code='last_position = {"x": 1, "y": 4}\nleft = 3\nold_x = last_position["x"]\nold_y = last_position["y"]\nprint(old_x < left)\nprint(max(left, old_x + 1))\nprint(max(left, old_y + 1))',
                output="True\n3\n5",
                check="Which old occurrence is already irrelevant to the window beginning at index 3?"),
        section("Explain complexity in terms of actual work",
                "Define n before saying O(n): it might be characters, records, or vertices. One full scan performs work proportional to n when its inner operations have constant average cost. A nested loop is not automatically O(n²); count how often each index advances. A sliding boundary that only moves forward can advance at most n times.\n\nDictionary and set membership have constant average-time behavior in the usual analysis, while sorting a general list costs O(n log n). Keeping a map can use O(n) extra space in the worst case. A concise expression can still hide a full scan or copy, so inspect slicing and repeated sorting. State assumptions and the clearest correct approach before proposing an optimization.",
                check="If you sort records and then take a small slice, does the slice remove the cost of sorting all records?"),
        section("Validation and normalization are separate stages",
                "Validation asks whether data meets the contract; normalization converts equivalent accepted representations into one form. A title might be accepted only as a nonempty stripped string, then normalized to lowercase. Check type before .strip, and decide whether bool is excluded from integer fields. A valid zero must not disappear through a truthy fallback.\n\nKeep different policies distinct: first wins, latest wins, and sum by key describe different aggregations. A timestamp is a time representation, not a duration; the task may supply comparable integers without asking you to parse real calendar dates. Read the specified rule rather than inferring it from one example.",
                code='raw_label = "  Observatory "\nnormalized = raw_label.strip().lower()\nquantity = 0\nprint(normalized)\nprint(type(quantity) is int)\nprint(quantity >= 0)',
                output="observatory\nTrue\nTrue",
                check="At which stage should an all-whitespace label be rejected?"),
        section("A useful test matrix is small but deliberate",
                "For an unseen task, choose tests from the contract instead of generating many arbitrary inputs. Cover an ordinary result, empty input, a precise boundary, invalid data where relevant, duplicates, ties, and mutation policy. Not every task needs every category; explain which apply.\n\nStore a separate snapshot before calling a function when testing input preservation. A shallow list copy shares nested dictionaries, so nested mutation tests may need copy.deepcopy or a deliberately reconstructed expected value. Use independent expected results calculated by hand; calling the function itself to compute the expected value would merely compare the implementation with itself.",
                code='from copy import deepcopy\noriginal = [{"name": "lamp", "count": 2}]\nsnapshot = deepcopy(original)\noriginal[0]["count"] = 9\nprint(snapshot[0]["count"])\nprint(original == snapshot)',
                output="2\nFalse",
                check="Why would original.copy() fail to preserve the nested count in this example?"),
        section("Existing-code debugging is a separate skill",
                "An independent assessment should include reading and changing unfamiliar code. Reproduce one bug with the smallest input, read the traceback or failed assertion, identify the responsible boundary, and add a test before fixing it. Make a focused change and rerun related tests. Preserve a reproduction and explain the cause, not just the final patch.\n\nFor SQL practice, use the chapter 16 tools in a separate temporary database: write a join, aggregate, and filtering query, then explain whether duplicate joined rows affect counts. For Git, use a focused branch, inspect staged changes, and write a reviewable PR. These demonstrate different abilities from solving one Python function.",
                check="Can you show a test failing for the original bug and passing after your change?"),
        section("A timed take-home needs priorities and a handoff",
                "Timebox the rehearsal to the lab's stated duration. First capture acceptance examples, then implement a narrow complete workflow, then tests, then run instructions. Avoid spending the whole session on packaging or appearance while the main behavior is unfinished. Keep a short list of deliberate omissions.\n\nYour handoff should say what works, which command demonstrates it, which tests passed, assumptions, limitations, and help used. If time ends, a documented coherent subset is more useful than broad claims over half-finished features. Do not silently change acceptance criteria to make the result appear complete.",
                check="Which single end-to-end behavior will you finish before adding optional features?"),
        section("Use evidence to choose the next practice session",
                "Readiness is a collection of demonstrated skills, not a completion percentage or employment guarantee. Ask whether you can start a small program, explain a loop, test a boundary, debug an unfamiliar error, use Git, query data, and hand a project to another person. Repeat an unfamiliar variation after a delay to separate understanding from recent memory.\n\nExplain one project aloud in two minutes: problem, approach, a tradeoff, a failure, and verification. If you cannot explain a generated component, mark that as a learning gap and revisit it. Use the weakest observed skill to plan the next week; role-specific frontend, data, embedded, or engine work may need further learning beyond this foundation.",
                check="What specific recent attempt shows your weakest skill, and what small independent task would exercise it next?"),
    ],
}
